1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,324
This project asks how to predict a movie rating and
how uncertain that prediction is.

2
00:00:05,546 --> 00:00:09,942
MovieLens One M contains just over one million
ratings. Only about four and a half percent of

3
00:00:09,942 --> 00:00:14,339
possible user and movie pairs are observed.

4
00:00:14,705 --> 00:00:19,406
Our model predicts the probability of each star
rating. We test repeated inference passes and an

5
00:00:19,406 --> 00:00:24,108
undirected graph that connects similar movies.

6
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:29,010
Each user and movie has an eight dimensional latent
vector. The coordinates are learned from ratings, so

7
00:00:29,010 --> 00:00:33,520
they do not have fixed genre names.

8
00:00:33,896 --> 00:00:37,752
Their dot product, plus biases, gives a score.
Thresholds convert a noisy score into one of five

9
00:00:37,752 --> 00:00:41,609
star ratings.

10
00:00:41,931 --> 00:00:46,197
Given the latent traits and biases, ratings are
independent. They are related marginally because many

11
00:00:46,197 --> 00:00:50,463
ratings share the same traits.

12
00:00:50,833 --> 00:00:57,482
Assumed density filtering visits each rating once and
approximates its effect with Gaussian messages.

13
00:00:57,759 --> 00:01:03,725
One pass of expectation propagation is identical in
our implementation. Later passes remove the old

14
00:01:03,725 --> 00:01:09,690
contribution, recompute it using the remaining
evidence, and damp the update.

15
00:01:10,187 --> 00:01:14,889
Repeated passes improve prediction error here, but
increase training time. The Gaussian approximation

16
00:01:14,889 --> 00:01:19,591
still misses some posterior dependencies.

17
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,376
The Markov network places an undirected edge between
movies with similar rating patterns or genres.

18
00:01:26,642 --> 00:01:31,688
The Gaussian prior penalizes differences between
connected movie vectors. When a movie has few

19
00:01:31,688 --> 00:01:36,734
ratings, a related movie can help stabilize its
vector.

20
00:01:37,154 --> 00:01:42,153
We fit maximum a posteriori estimates. Setting gamma
to zero removes the edge penalty while keeping the

21
00:01:42,153 --> 00:01:47,152
same fitting method. That is our graph ablation.

22
00:01:47,583 --> 00:01:54,992
For each of five random seeds, all methods share the
same training, validation, and test observations.

23
00:01:55,301 --> 00:01:59,465
Only training ratings build the graph. Validation
chooses settings, and test ratings are used only for

24
00:01:59,465 --> 00:02:03,629
evaluation.

25
00:02:03,976 --> 00:02:08,447
We also reran the full seed forty two split. All one
hundred forty six compared scores and settings

26
00:02:08,447 --> 00:02:12,918
matched, with timings excluded.

27
00:02:13,292 --> 00:02:17,796
Ten expectation propagation passes reduce average
root mean squared error from about point eight nine

28
00:02:17,796 --> 00:02:22,300
eight to point eight five five.

29
00:02:22,675 --> 00:02:26,920
The graph model scores point eight four nine nine.
Compared with the same model without the graph, the

30
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:31,164
gain is about six ten thousandths.

31
00:02:31,518 --> 00:02:36,721
Four of five within split bootstrap intervals favor
the graph; the fifth includes zero. These splits

32
00:02:36,721 --> 00:02:41,924
overlap, so we do not treat them as independent
replications.

33
00:02:42,357 --> 00:02:46,069
Graph MAP has the lowest average log loss, but its
calibration error is slightly higher than graph free

34
00:02:46,069 --> 00:02:49,782
MAP.

35
00:02:50,125 --> 00:02:55,131
We kept only one quarter of the training ratings in
one experiment. In another, we changed twenty percent

36
00:02:55,131 --> 00:03:00,137
of the training stars and kept the test labels clean.

37
00:03:00,554 --> 00:03:05,202
The graph advantage grows under both conditions. This
is consistent with neighboring movies sharing useful

38
00:03:05,202 --> 00:03:09,850
evidence when individual observations are weak.

39
00:03:10,238 --> 00:03:15,558
Cold users remain difficult because movie edges do
not provide user preferences. Similarity can also be

40
00:03:15,558 --> 00:03:20,879
misleading if an edge connects unrelated movies.

41
00:03:21,333 --> 00:03:28,067
Microsoft Research Matchbox motivates our approximate
inference. PreRec addresses a different problem,

42
00:03:28,067 --> 00:03:34,800
cross domain ranking. Our benchmark does not
establish performance against either complete system.

43
00:03:35,361 --> 00:03:40,122
The main gain from repeated inference is clear. The
graph adds modest value on clean ratings and is most

44
00:03:40,122 --> 00:03:44,884
useful in the sparse and noisy settings we tested.

45
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:51,118
A next step is to test time ordered splits and
account for uncertainty in the graph model. The

46
00:03:51,118 --> 00:03:56,956
package contains the code, configurations, results,
and reproduction commands.
